well still to come on the show we'll be  hearing from the head of Ofsted plus  I've spoken to people on both sides of  the heated debate about HST but first  for three years Northern Ireland was  without a devolved government but after  a deal brokered by the UK and Irish  governments an executive and assembly  was reformed three weeks ago we're  joined now by the First Minister of  Northern Ireland Arlene Foster thank you  very much for being on the program at  Miss posture  well we're now officially out of the  European Union we heard a little earlier  from the Foreign Secretary Dominic Rob  being pretty clear that the government  wants to diverge from the EU are you  worried that this could mean more checks  between Northern Ireland and the rest of  the UK  well good morning Sophie yes we have  left the European Union but  unfortunately for those of us who live  in Northern Ireland we are going to be  still the subject to single market  regulations and it's important now for  us to get clarity on what that actually  means for us in Northern Ireland in  relation to future trade deals I'm  indeed what's going to happen when goods  are coming from Great Britain into  Northern Ireland because of course we  rely heavily on goods coming from Great  Britain and Northern Ireland I think  about 70% of all the goods that come  over into Northern Ireland come from  Great Britain and of course we're  concerned about that we worry about the  fact that it might reduce our choice in  terms of consumer choice in Northern  Ireland we're worried about price and  the fact that it may put up prices in  Northern Ireland and of course the very  fundamental issue of having checks  internally within a country is of great  concern to me as a unionist I mean Boris  Johnson said that there won't be checks  but of course if he does really want to  diverge from the EU to the extent that  we're led to believe it's quite hard to  see how that could happen I mean do you  trust Boris Johnson on 