and  why do you think it took so long to get  to this point well that's a very good  question for our part we were always  prepared to go back into government  again and I suppose we could spend the  rest of the interview talking about what  happened over this past three years but  for for me it's important that we look  forward that we deal with all of the  challenges that we have because we  haven't had a government here for three  years principally those arise in our  national health service the fact that we  have the longest waiting lists anywhere  in the United Kingdom and we need to  deal with those issues and to focus on  what we have in front of us instead of  looking backwards I mean the deal that  restored and power-sharing all state  included this promise by the Westminster  government to implement something called  the Stormont house agreement now that's  an agreement from 2014  where the government commits to setting  up this historical investigations unit  to investigate deaths during the  Troubles au worries that this could mean  fresh investigations into British troops  well I think the Stormont house  agreement is five years old now nearly  six years old it's important that we  look at all of the consultation  responses that came in from various  victims groups so to see what they want  and I think we need to revisit the  Stormont house agreement because what  has been proposed and we made this clear  in our consultation response at the time  what is being proposed is not acceptable  I think to the victims grips here and  it's certainly not acceptable to us so  there needs to be a relook at the  Stormont house agreement particularly in  relation to the remit of the historical  institution the hiu which is there to  look at historical cases do UK  government is perhaps under estimating  how serious this could potentially be no  I think they're very much aware of how  serious this issue is this is a legacy  issue from 1990 at the Belfast agreement  victims were not treated well in the  Belfast agreement that was left as an  open wouldn't we now have been left with  nearly 22 years later we're still  dealing with these issues there is a  need to deal with these issues I  absolutely agree with that but it has to  be done in a way that recognizes  fairness and recognises what actually  happened here in Northern Ireland over  3540 years the fact that we did have a  terrorist campaign and there were so  many innocent victims as a result of  that I mean Boris Johnson says he won't  will end vexatious claims do you trust  him that he'll be able to do that well I  hope that he will be able to do that but  I hope that he does it in a way that  recognizes not just soldiers and British  soldiers who live on the mainland but  also he takes into account those people  who continue to live here in Northern  Ireland security force members who  continue to live here in Northern  Ireland need to be protected as well  from vexatious claims I think that is  very important so we will be continuing  the conversation with government about  legacy issues we'll be continuing the  conversation obviously an executive  about these issues as well now that the  executive is back but as I say this is a  running sore that runs from 1998 and at  some  thing that does need to be sorted out  now I've been talking of course about  the Duvall government the restoration or  fire in Northern Ireland and of course  the executive collapsed in part the  course of this renewable energy scheme  where a businesses were encouraged to  switch to renewable energy but it  actually ended up wasting 500 million  pounds of taxpayers money is it right  that three years later you're back as  First Minister  are you sorry well of course there is a  public inquiry which hasn't yet come to  the fore that public inquiry will bring  forward a report and we will learn and  adapt from that public inquiry report I  think it's important that we do learn  the lessons from that area but you know  at the time that the executive collect  collapsed there were wild and frenzied  allegations around about a number of  people I think it's important that we  have the report from the public inquiry  which will deal with all of those issues  and we look forward to that coming in  the near future  500 million pounds a lot of money they  are you not sorry well it's not actually  we're on an understand situation in  relation to the Renewable Heat Incentive  scheme because we took I mean when  people talk about five hundred thousand  pounds up was the projected figure if we  hadn't have intervened and dealt with  the issue we did deal with the issue and  no there isn't an overspend in fact  there is an understand in relation to  this scheme so I think when people throw  about those sorts of figures they really  need to look at the fact the fact was  that was a projected amount of money  that's now not going to happen and in  fact we're in an understand situation  what's the Westminster Government okay  now we could be looking at HS - a little  bit later in the programme Boris Johnson  has been promising some big  infrastructure spending one of the ideas  that he didn't meet once was this idea  of a bridge linking Northern Ireland to  the UK mainland is that something that  you'd like him to be considering well I  think it is important that we look at  infrastructure projects and not only  look at them but actually look at how  they're delivered and one of the  difficulties with HS two is the fact  that it is overrun spectacularly and  that's not unusual unfortunately with  infrastructure projects and there's a  need to look at how those are delivered  right across the United Kingdom but if  we are to look at rebalancing the United  Kingdom  because of course there are only three  regions of the UK economy which are net  contributors to the economy and those  are all in one part of the UK down in  the southeastern on them if we are to  rebalance the economy across the United  Kingdom then there needs to be  interventions and one of those  interventions is to have better  infrastructure so I support the  government in putting money into  infrastructure but it has to be done in  a way that we get value for money and it  has to be done in a way that it's  project managed in the correct fashion  so do you like no I do for bridge then  well I think it should be scoped out I  think we should be ambitious in relation  to all of our infrastructure projects I  don't think it's the complete priority  for the Northern Ireland Executive F I'm  entirely honest but I think that we  should look at ambitious projects  because in the past when we have  ambitious projects those were the ones  that made the the difference if you like  to our economy okay Arlene Foster thank  you very much for coming on the program  this morning thank you thank you

